 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(R \cdot (N_{coarse} + N_{fine}))$ network queries per ray batch, where $R$ is the number of rays. The sorting step adds $O(R \cdot N \log N)$ [S5].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(R \cdot N_{fine})$ to store intermediate tensors for the fine pass [S5].
 - **Performance:** Significantly higher quality than single-stage uniform sampling, particularly for complex geometry and view-dependent effects, at the cost of roughly double the network queries per ray [S5].
 - **Strengths:** Differentiable end-to-end, allowing optimization from images alone; handles view-dependent appearance via directional encoding; hierarchical sampling efficiently allocates samples to dense regions [S1, S5].
-- **Limitations:** Requires many network queries (slow rendering without acceleration structures like KD-trees or voxel grids); sensitive to near/far bounds; prone to "floaters" (noise in empty space) if not regularized or if near/far are poorly estimated [S1, S5].
-- **When to use:** When high-fidelity novel view synthesis is required and training time is acceptable. Not suitable for real-time rendering without further acceleration (e.g., [3D Gaussian Splatting](791-distance-color-blending.md) or voxel-based acceleration).
+- **Limitations:** The basic implementation is inefficient and does not converge to a sufficiently high-resolution representation for complex scenes without hierarchical refinement [S1].
 
 ## Background
-NeRF (Neural Radiance Fields) was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1]. It represents a scene as a continuous 5D function mapping spatial coordinates and viewing directions to volume density and emitted color. The method is trained by minimizing the difference between rendered images and ground truth images using gradient descent, leveraging the differentiability of the volume rendering integral [S1]. The coarse-to-fine sampling strategy was introduced to address the inefficiency and aliasing artifacts of uniform sampling, allowing the model to focus computational resources on regions with high density [S5].
